WEBVTT

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.660
Welcome to our Product Engineering 2
presentation.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.200
I would like to introduce
our smart robotic surface,

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.600
which is designed to mimic human skin.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.640
First, let’s look at how the system is built.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.700
Here is the core of the system:
five different layers,

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.060
two red and three black.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.460
Let’s look at these in more detail.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.020
We’ll start with the middle black layer.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.960
Here we have a piece of black denim

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.740
with eight conductive yarn tracks
sewn onto it in a zigzag pattern.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:43.640
These are connected to our transmitter board.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.020
It sends a pulse along each
of the individual wires in turn.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.380
These pulses are then picked up
by the two receiver layers:

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.740
the top and bottom layers.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.880
Like the middle layer,
these also have eight sewn wire tracks,

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.120
but at right angles to the middle ones.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:10.440
When pressure on the skin
compresses the intermediate layer—

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.260
the compression layer—

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.780
the two wires touch at one point.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:20.540
This gives us an 8 × 8 grid:
64 possible pressure points.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.940
The receiver layers pick up the signal
here at the receiver board.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.780
We then read it using an Arduino Uno.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.960
Let’s see what happens
when we swap the intermediate layers.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:38.140
We can simply swap the lower layer
using our smart press-stud system.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:41.840
We undo the layer and remove it.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.320
This time, we use a 25% layer
instead of a 15% one.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.560
This has a much denser mesh.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.080
Now we can fit it back in
using the press studs.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.000
The same on the other side.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:14.150
When we press it now,
the upper contact stays the same.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:17.890
But we have to press much harder
to activate the lower contact.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:21.390
This would let us measure
different pressure levels.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.510
We could extend this
with any number of layers

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.990
to measure different pressure levels.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:31.070
Of course, this is only a prototype
and a feasibility study.

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:34.770
But to show what this could look like
on a robot in the future:

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:39.610
if the skin were made more flexible,
more reliable and larger,

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:46.030
we could dress the robotic arm
in a kind of jeans—a skin.

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:50.490
The product also works when bent,
as we can see here.

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:53.850
When I press on my arm,
the contact is detected,

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:55.700
including firm contact.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.600
Thank you for watching.
See you next time!
